To generate a testbench that isolates the correct mutant among the buggy versions, we need to ensure that the testbench comprehensively checks the combinatorial logic of the binary-to-Gray code converter. The testbench should cover all possible input combinations and verify that the output matches the expected Gray code. Additionally, we need to ensure that the testbench adheres to the provided simulation rules, including dynamic verification and proper reporting.

[[ ## testbench_code ## ]]
```verilog
module tb_enc_bin2gray;

  // Inputs
  reg [9:0] bin;

  // Outputs
  wire [9:0] gray;

  // Instantiate the Unit Under Test (UUT)
  enc_bin2gray uut (
    .bin(bin),
    .gray(gray)
  );

  // Golden Value for dynamic verification
  reg [9:0] golden_gray;

  // Testbench process
  initial begin
    // Initialize Inputs
    bin = 10'b0;

    // Wait 100 ns for global reset to finish
    #100;

    // Loop through all possible 10-bit binary inputs
    for (integer i = 0; i < 1024; i = i + 1) begin
      bin = i;
      golden_gray = bin ^ (bin >> 1);
      #10;
      if (gray !== golden_gray) begin
        $display("TEST FAILED at bin = %b, expected %b, got %b", bin, golden_gray, gray);
        $finish;
      end
    end

    // If all checks pass, print TEST PASSED
    $display("TEST PASSED");
    $finish;
  end

endmodule
```

